"{\"start\":\"ORIENTATIONHow to use this companionA lecture-by-lecture deep guide to the ETH Zürich Robot Learning course, written for someone who already knows machine learning and mathematics — and is building the robotics and RL layers on top.What this is. A self-contained study site for all twelve weeks of Robot Learning: From Fundamentals to Foundation Models (Oier Mees, ETH Zürich, Spring 2026). For each lecture you get: the conceptual core with full mathematical derivations, a Bridge panel that maps each new idea onto something you already know from ML, mathematics, or actuarial science, the week's discussion papers decoded, a self-check quiz, and direct links to the official slides, recordings, and homework.What this is not. I built this from the official course syllabus, the week-by-week paper lists, and deep treatments of each topic — not from the slide decks themselves (they're image-based PDFs). So the coverage matches what the course covers, but Mees's specific examples, jokes, and slide-level ordering live in the videos. Treat this site as the textbook layer underneath the lectures: watch the video, then come here to make the math and the context stick — or read here first so the lecture feels like review.Suggested loop per lecture1. Skim the lecture section here (15 min) → 2. Watch the recording at 1.25–1.5× → 3. Return here for the derivations and Bridge panels → 4. Read the abstract + intro + figures of each discussion paper → 5. Take the quiz → 6. Do the matching homework if one exists → 7. Mark complete.Interactive labsThroughout the lectures you'll find panels tagged INTERACTIVE — small visual sandboxes in the spirit of 3Blue1Brown for the ideas that resist words. Watch value iteration propagate across a grid, watch a policy climb a reward landscape from reward alone, drag the PPO clip until the trust region appears, watch noise resolve into two valid actions. Play with the sliders — the intuition they build is the whole point.Notation key & glossary — open this once, refer back as neededA note on s vs o. Throughout, s is the state (a complete description of the situation) and o is the observation (what the robot's sensors actually report). In a fully-observed problem they're the same; in the real world the robot sees o and must infer what it needs about s. RL theory is written in s; imitation and generative-policy sections often write o because a camera image is the input. When you see π(a∣s) and π(a∣o), read them as the same idea — map situation to action — under different observability assumptions.Symbols. E[⋅] expectation (probability-weighted average) · ∇θ​ gradient w.r.t. parameters θ · ∼ \\\"is sampled from\\\" · argmaxa​ the action achieving the maximum · ∫ integral (continuous sum) · ⊙ element-wise product · γ discount factor · α learning rate / step size · πθ​ policy (the network) · τ a trajectory (one episode's states+actions).policy πThe decision rule mapping state/observation to an action (or a distribution over actions). The thing we ultimately want.rollout / trajectory τOne run of the policy in the environment: the sequence (s0​,a0​,r0​,s1​,a1​,…) it produces.return GₜThe (discounted) sum of rewards from time t onward — the quantity every RL method is trying to make large.value V(s) / action-value Q(s,a)Expected return from a state (V), or from taking an action in a state then following the policy (Q). \\\"How good is it here?\\\"advantage A(s,a)Q(s,a)−V(s): how much better this action is than the policy's average here. The signal modern policy gradients actually use.on-policy vs off-policyOn-policy: you may only learn from data the current policy generated (PPO). Off-policy: you can reuse old data or others' data (Q-learning, SAC) — what makes replay buffers legal. This distinction gates half of Lectures 4–5.episodic vs continuingTasks that end (an episode: grasp the mug, done) vs. tasks that run forever (balance indefinitely). Discounting γ keeps returns finite in the continuing case.bootstrappingUpdating an estimate using your own current estimate (TD's r+γV(s′)). Lower variance, but biased while you're still wrong.replay bufferA stored pool of past transitions (s,a,r,s′) sampled in random minibatches — decorrelates updates and reuses experience. Only valid for off-policy methods.critic / actorThe critic is a network that estimates value (it judges); the actor is the policy (it acts). Actor-critic methods run both in a loop.score function ∇log πThe gradient of log-probability of an action; the core object REINFORCE multiplies by return. Its expectation is zero — the fact behind baselines.reparameterization trickWriting a sample as a differentiable function of noise (a=μ+σε) so gradients flow through it. The low-variance alternative to the score function; powers SAC and VAEs.chunking / receding horizonPredicting a short sequence of future actions at once, executing the first few, then re-planning. Borrowed from MPC; central to ACT, Diffusion Policy, π0.The VLA lineage (Lecture 9), decoded once. The acronym storm in L9 is really one family tree:VLM → VLAA Vision-Language Model (image+text in, text out) becomes a Vision-Language-Action model when you let its output vocabulary include motor actions. That one move is the whole paradigm.LfP / MCILLearning from Play / Multi-Context Imitation Learning: collect unscripted teleoperated play, relabel windows in hindsight as goal-reaching demos, train one goal-conditioned policy (goal = image or sentence). Mees's research line.CALVINMees's benchmark for long-horizon, language-conditioned manipulation — chains of instructions from onboard sensing.GatoOne transformer, 600+ tasks, everything serialized to tokens — the maximalist \\\"generality by tokenization\\\" thesis.RT-1 / RT-2Robotics Transformer: RT-1 scaled within one robot (actions as 256 discrete bins); RT-2 fine-tuned a pretrained VLM to emit those action tokens, importing web semantics into control.OXEOpen X-Embodiment: a pooled corpus of 1M+ trajectories from 22 robot types; proved pooling across different robot bodies helps each one (positive cross-embodiment transfer).Octo / OpenVLAOpen generalist policies trained on OXE — Octo with a diffusion/flow action head, OpenVLA a 7B open re-creation of RT-2 (Mees among the authors) adaptable via LoRA.π0 / π0.5 / π*0.6Physical Intelligence's line: π0 = VLM + flow-matching action expert for 50 Hz control; π0.5 = open-world generalization via heterogeneous co-training + language subtask inference; π*0.6 = RL-from-experience (RECAP: advantage-conditioned policy) that improves past demonstration quality.LoRALow-Rank Adaptation: fine-tune a big model by training tiny low-rank weight deltas — cheap downstream adaptation to a new robot/task on consumer GPUs.Hide the finance / actuarial bridges The \\\"Bridge\\\" panels map ideas onto an ML + actuarial background. If that framing isn't yours, hide them — everything else stands alone.What you bring vs. what you're buildingYour background is unusually well-suited to this course, more than you might think. Here's the honest inventory:You already haveWhere it pays offSupervised learning, losses, gradients, backprop, overfittingImitation learning (L3) is supervised learning; every policy is a neural net trained by gradient descentProbability, expectations, conditional distributionsEvery single derivation in L4–L6. RL is applied probability.Markov chains (actuarial multi-state models, transition matrices)An MDP is a Markov chain you get to steer. You know 70% of L2 already.Discounting, expected present value, reserves, recursion relationsThis is the secret weapon. The Bellman equation is an actuarial recursion. See the Primer.Monte Carlo simulation, stochastic scenariosPolicy evaluation, REINFORCE, and every \\\"rollout\\\" in the courseGenerative models if you've touched VAEs/diffusionL6 directly; if not, L6 builds them from scratchYou're missingWhere this site fills itRobotics: kinematics, dynamics, control loops, sensors, simThe Primer, Part A + L2RL: the agent–environment loop, value functions, the algorithm zooThe Primer, Part B + L2, L4, L5The robot-learning literature and its lineage (who did what, why it mattered)\\\"The papers, decoded\\\" in every lecture + L9–L11The course in one mapThe twelve weeks tell a single story: how do we get robots to acquire skills from data instead of hand-written equations — and what happens when we scale that up?L01–L02The problem & the formalismWhy robots are hard, why learning, and the MDP language everything else is written in.L03Copy the expertImitation learning: the simplest idea, its failure mode (compounding errors), and the fixes.L04–L05Learn from rewardValue-based RL, then policy gradients, PPO, SAC — the engines of modern robot RL.L06–L07Better policy classesDiffusion policies and transformers: treat action generation as generative modeling and sequence modeling.L08Learn the world itselfWorld models: learn dynamics, then plan or train inside the dream.L09–L10Foundation modelsVision-Language-Action models, generalist policies, LLM reasoning and test-time compute for robots.L11–L12The frontierOpen problems, three competing worldviews, and perspectives from the people who built the field.Official course materialsCourse-wide linksPageOfficial course website (syllabus, slides, schedule)YouTubeMain lecture playlist (Oier Mees)CodeCourse GitHub: homework 1–4 (PyTorch, Control/MDPs, Imitation, RL)RecordingsETH video portal (all lecture recordings)Progress you mark here is saved when this page runs inside Claude (and lives only in your account's artifact storage). If you download the file and open it locally, progress lasts for the session only.Ready? Start with the Primer — it's the foundation everything else stands on.NEXT: THE PRIMER →\",\"primer\":\"MODULE 00The Primer: your missing backgroundThree parts: what a robot actually is (Part A), the decision-making skeleton all twelve lectures hang on (Part B), and a Rosetta stone from actuarial mathematics to reinforcement learning (Part C). Read this before Lecture 1; revisit it constantly.PART AWhat a robot actually isStrip away the science fiction. A robot is sensors + actuators + a computer, wired into a feedback loop with the physical world. The computer reads sensors, decides, commands actuators, and the world responds — many times per second, forever. Everything in this course is about what runs inside that loop. ┌──────────────────────────────┐ │ WORLD │ │ (objects, physics, people) │ └───────┬──────────────▲───────┘ │ │ observations actions (camera, joints, (motor torques, force, touch) target poses) │ │ ┌───────▼──────────────┴───────┐ │ POLICY π(a|o) │ │ the thing this course │ │ teaches you how to LEARN │ └──────────────────────────────┘FIG. P1 — The perception–action loop. Classical robotics fills the box with hand-derived models; robot learning fills it with a trained neural network.State: where the robot is, in numbersA robot arm with 7 motors (\\\"joints\\\") is described at any instant by its joint angles q∈R7 and joint velocities q˙​∈R7. The vector q lives in configuration space — the space of all possible poses. The full physical state is x=(q,q˙​): like in classical mechanics, position + velocity determines the future (given the inputs).Kinematics: geometry without forcesForward kinematics is a known, fixed function from joint angles to where the hand (the \\\"end-effector\\\") is in 3D space: pee​=f(q). It's just trigonometry chained along the arm's links — easy. Inverse kinematics asks the reverse: which q puts my hand at a desired p? That's solving a nonlinear system, often with many or no solutions. The derivative of forward kinematics is the Jacobian J(q)=∂f/∂q, giving the velocity relation p˙​ee​=J(q)q˙​ — the workhorse of classical arm control.Feel configuration space: drive an arm by its joint anglesThe abstract phrase \\\"the policy outputs joint angles q\\\" becomes obvious the moment you see it. Below is a 2-joint arm. You don't move the hand directly — you set the two angles, and forward kinematics decides where the hand lands. Sweep them and watch the reachable workspace fill in. Two things to notice: most hand positions are reachable by two different angle combinations (that's why inverse kinematics has multiple solutions), and a small change in angle near full extension moves the hand a lot (that's the Jacobian stretching). This is the space every learned policy is really acting in.INTERACTIVE Forward kinematics: joint angles → hand positionYou control q=(θ1​,θ2​); the hand position pee​=f(q) follows. “Trace workspace” sweeps the joints to draw every reachable point — the arm's configuration space made visible. The action space of a real policy is exactly these angles, not the Cartesian hand position you might expect.M(q)q¨​+C(q,q˙​)q˙​+g(q)=τM is the configuration-dependent inertia matrix, C collects Coriolis/centrifugal terms, g is gravity, and τ is the vector of motor torques you command. If you knew this model perfectly, control would be (almost) calculus. The catch: the moment the robot touches anything — a mug, the floor, a door handle — you add contact forces and friction, which are discontinuous, hard to measure, and brutally hard to model. Contact is the single biggest reason manipulation resists classical methods and motivates learning.The control stack: two loops at two speedsReal systems are layered. A low-level controller runs at 100–1000 Hz keeping motors at commanded targets — usually some flavor of PID (proportional–integral–derivative: push back proportionally to error, its integral, and its derivative). On top, the policy — the learned part — runs at 1–60 Hz, outputting either target joint positions, end-effector poses, or raw torques. When a paper says the policy outputs \\\"actions,\\\" ask: actions at which level of this stack? It changes the difficulty of the learning problem enormously.Sensors, simulation, and the gapTypical observations: RGB camera images, depth images, proprioception (the robot's own joint angles/velocities — its sense of its body), sometimes force/torque or tactile sensing. Note what's missing: the robot never observes \\\"the mug's pose\\\" directly; it sees pixels. Much of robot learning is implicitly learning perception.Because real robots are slow, expensive, and breakable, the field leans hard on physics simulators — MuJoCo, Isaac Gym/Isaac Lab (GPU-parallelized, thousands of simultaneous robots), PyBullet. Simulation is cheap data; the price is the sim-to-real gap: simulated friction, contacts, latencies, and images never quite match reality, so policies that ace simulation can fail on hardware. Closing that gap (L5) is a discipline of its own.Moravec's paradox — the course's founding observation Things hard for humans (chess, calculus, Go) turned out easy for computers; things trivial for a toddler (picking up a toy, walking on gravel) remain at the frontier of robotics. Evolution spent billions of years optimizing sensorimotor skill and a few millennia on symbolic reasoning — so our intuitions about what's \\\"hard\\\" are exactly backwards. Keep this in mind every time a task in this course looks mundane.PART BThe decision-making skeletonHere is the formal frame that Lectures 2–10 all share. An agent interacts with an environment in discrete time steps. At step t: the agent sees state st​, picks action at​∼π(⋅∣st​), the environment returns reward rt​ and next state st+1​∼P(⋅∣st​,at​). This repeats, producing a trajectory (or \\\"rollout\\\" / \\\"episode\\\") τ=(s0​,a0​,r0​,s1​,a1​,r1​,…).The agent's objective is the expected return — discounted cumulative reward:Gt​=rt​+γrt+1​+γ2rt+2​+⋯=k=0∑∞​γkrt+k​,γ∈[0,1)Two functions summarize \\\"how good things are,\\\" and the entire field is built on them:Vπ(s)=Eπ​[Gt​∣st​=s]Qπ(s,a)=Eπ​[Gt​∣st​=s,at​=a]Vπ is the expected return from state s if you follow policy π; Qπ is the same but with the first action pinned to a. Their relationship: Vπ(s)=Ea∼π​[Qπ(s,a)]. The advantage Aπ(s,a)=Qπ(s,a)−Vπ(s) measures how much better action a is than π's average behavior — it will star in Lecture 5.Because returns are recursive (Gt​=rt​+γGt+1​), value functions obey the Bellman equation:Vπ(s)=Ea∼π,s′∼P​[r(s,a)+γVπ(s′)]Memorize the shape: value now = expected immediate reward + discounted expected value next. You will see it forty times in this course wearing different costumes.The three families of solutionsValue-based (L4): learn Q, then act by argmaxa​Q(s,a). The policy is implicit.Policy-based (L5): parameterize πθ​ directly and do gradient ascent on expected return. Handles continuous actions natively — which is why robotics lives here.Model-based (L8): learn the dynamics P(s′∣s,a) itself, then plan or train inside the learned model.Two distinctions you must keep straight from day one. On-policy vs. off-policy: does the algorithm require fresh data from the current policy (PPO: yes), or can it learn from any data, including old or other-policy data (Q-learning, SAC: yes, via a replay buffer)? Exploration vs. exploitation: unlike supervised learning, the agent chooses its own training distribution — act greedily too early and you never see the data that would teach you better.Going deeper: partial observability (POMDPs) — why robots don't quite live in MDPsThe MDP assumes the agent sees the true state st​. A camera-driven robot sees an observation ot​ (pixels) that incompletely reflects state — occlusions, unseen object properties like mass or friction, things behind the robot. Formally this is a POMDP, where the optimal agent maintains a belief bt​=p(st​∣o1:t​,a1:t−1​) — a posterior over states given history. Exact belief tracking is intractable, so in practice deep RL approximates it by feeding the policy a history: stacked recent frames, a recurrent network's hidden state, or a transformer's context window. When you reach Lecture 7 (sequence models) and Lecture 8 (world models with latent states), recognize them as learned, approximate belief states. The math of this course is written for MDPs; the engineering is forever managing the gap.Feel the discount before you trust itBefore moving on, build intuition for the one free parameter in all of RL. The return weights a reward k steps away by γk. Drag γ below and watch two things at once: how fast the future fades, and the total weight ∑k​γk=1/(1−γ) — which doubles as an effective planning horizon. At γ=0.99 the agent effectively looks ~100 steps ahead; at γ=0.9, only ~10. This single number silently sets how far-sighted every algorithm in the course is — and it's the same v=1/(1+i) you discount cash flows with.INTERACTIVE How the discount factor shapes the horizonEach orange bar is the weight γk on the reward k steps ahead. The dashed cyan line marks the effective horizon 1/(1−γ); the orange number is the total discounted weight of an infinite future.PART CThe actuarial Rosetta stoneHere's the part nobody will tell you in lecture: you have been doing dynamic programming on Markov processes with discounting your entire actuarial career. The vocabulary differs; the mathematics is the same. The clearest example — compare the actuarial recursion for a whole-life annuity-due with the Bellman equation:a¨x​=1+vpx​a¨x+1​⟺Vπ(s)=r(s)+γs′∑​P(s′∣s)Vπ(s′)Read the left side as an RL problem: the \\\"state\\\" is being alive at age x; the \\\"reward\\\" is the payment of 1 each period; the \\\"discount factor\\\" is v=1/(1+i); the \\\"transition\\\" is surviving to age x+1 with probability px​; death is an absorbing terminal state with value 0. An annuity value is a value function. A reserve is a value function. You have computed Vπ thousands of times.Bridge · Actuarial science → Reinforcement learningDiscount factor v=1/(1+i), time value of money→γ — same algebra; interpretation adds \\\"prefer reward sooner\\\" and \\\"keep infinite sums finite\\\"EPV of future cash flows; reserves t​V→Value function Vπ(s) — expected discounted future reward from a stateMulti-state models (healthy/sick/dead), transition intensities→MDP transition kernel P(s′∣s,a) — with one upgrade: your actions change the transition probabilitiesRecursion relations (Thiele, annuity/insurance recursions)→Bellman equations — identical backward-recursive structureLife table given: compute EPVs from known qx​→\\\"Planning\\\" / dynamic programming (L2): model known, solve exactlyNo table — only experience data; experience studies, credibility updates→Model-free RL (L4–L5): estimate values from sampled trajectories; TD learning ≈ continuously credibility-weighting your estimate toward new experienceCredibility weight Z: blend prior estimate with observed experience→Learning rate α in V←(1−α)V+α[new estimate] — structurally the same blendStochastic scenario projection, model office runs→Monte Carlo rollouts — simulate trajectories, average discounted outcomesChoosing among policy designs to optimize an EPV objective→Control / policy optimization: search over decision rules π to maximize Vπ — the step actuarial work rarely takes, and RL's whole pointThe one genuinely new ingredient RL adds: the feedback of choice. In actuarial models you evaluate a fixed stochastic process. In RL, the process depends on a decision rule that you are simultaneously optimizing — and when learning on a real system, the data you collect depends on the policy you're still improving. That self-reference is the source of nearly every difficulty (and every interesting theorem) ahead.Bridge · Machine learning → Robot learningSupervised learning on i.i.d. pairs (x,y)→Behavioral cloning (L3) — same training, but at test time your own predictions generate the next inputs, breaking i.i.d.Covariate shift→Compounding errors / distribution shift — the central pathology of L3, induced by the agent itselfCross-entropy / MLE→maxθ​∑logπθ​(aexpert∣s) — BC is MLE of expert actions; REINFORCE reuses the same ∇logπ machineryVAEs, diffusion models for images→Generative policies (L6) — same math, but you sample actions conditioned on observationsAutoregressive LLMs, next-token prediction→L7 + L9: trajectories as token sequences; VLAs are language models whose vocabulary includes motor commandsPretrain → finetune, transfer learning→Robot foundation models (L9–L10): pretrain on internet + cross-robot data, finetune/prompt for your robot and taskIn the annuity-due recursion a¨x​=1+vpx​a¨x+1​, which RL quantity plays the role of px​?AThe discount factor γBThe transition probability to the next (non-terminal) stateCThe reward rDThe learning rate αB. Survival probability px​ is exactly P(s′∣s) into the \\\"alive at x+1\\\" state, with death as an absorbing state of value zero. v plays γ, the payment of 1 plays r, and the recursion itself is the Bellman equation for a fixed policy.A colleague says: \\\"RL is just supervised learning with a different loss.\\\" What's the most important thing that answer misses?ARL uses neural networks; supervised learning doesn't have toBRL requires discrete actionsCIn RL the agent's own behavior determines the data distribution it trains and is evaluated onDRL cannot use gradient descentC. The defining feature is closed-loop data: actions influence future states (and future training data), so the i.i.d. assumption fails, exploration matters, and credit must be assigned across time. Losses differ too, but that's downstream of this.Why does contact (touching objects, ground) specifically push robotics toward learning-based methods?AContact dynamics are discontinuous and friction-dominated, making accurate first-principles models extremely hardBContact makes the robot's state space infinite-dimensionalCSensors stop working during contactDContact violates the Markov propertyA. Free-space rigid-body motion follows clean equations Mq¨​+Cq˙​+g=τ. Contact adds stick/slip friction, impacts, and deformation — discontinuous, parameter-sensitive physics that's hard to identify. Learning sidesteps explicit modeling by training on experience of the real (or randomized simulated) thing.Mark Primer complete← BACKNEXT →\",\"l1\":\"LECTURE 01 · FEB 16Introduction to Robot LearningWhy the classical robotics pipeline hit a wall, why learning is the way through it, and the map of paradigms the rest of the course explores one by one.Prereqs none — start hereTime ~30 minWatch first the paradigm map (§1.3)Watch out for\\\"Robot learning\\\" is not \\\"deep learning that happens to run on a robot\\\" — the sequential, safety-critical, data-scarce setting changes which methods work.More learning/autonomy isn't automatically better; when the model is known, the classical pipeline (L2) often wins.1.1The classical pipeline and where it breaksRobotics worked for decades without learning. The classical recipe — sense → plan → act — perceives the world into an explicit state estimate (object poses, maps), plans a trajectory through it with model-based optimization, and executes with a feedback controller built on the dynamics equations from the Primer. This is how factory arms place millions of parts and how rockets land: when the environment is structured, instrumented, and modeled, classical methods are precise, verifiable, and unbeatable.The wall appears in open environments. Three compounding problems:Perception is the bottleneck. Going from pixels to \\\"the mug's 6D pose, its mass, its friction\\\" is itself an unsolved estimation problem in clutter, glare, and occlusion. Hand-built pipelines are brittle exactly where modern vision (deep networks) is strong.Contact-rich physics resists modeling. Inserting a plug, folding a shirt, walking on gravel — friction, deformation, impacts. Writing accurate equations per object and surface doesn't scale.The long tail. Homes and hospitals are not factories: every scene is slightly different. Engineering each case by hand has unbounded cost; we need systems that generalize — which is precisely what statistical learning is for.Hence the course's premise: replace hand-derived components with functions learned from data — sometimes just perception, increasingly the entire sensor-to-motor mapping (\\\"end-to-end\\\").1.2What makes robot learning different from the ML you knowIf learning works for images and language, why is robotics its own field? Four structural reasons — they will echo through every lecture:Closed loop. The policy's outputs change its future inputs. Errors compound; i.i.d. evaluation is meaningless; success is measured by executing in the world, not by held-out loss.No internet of robot data. Vision and NLP ride on billions of free web examples. There is no comparable corpus of robot sensorimotor experience; data must be generated by teleoperation, by simulation, or by the robot's own (risky, slow) trial and error. The data problem is the central economic fact of the field — most of L6–L11 is downstream of it.Physical cost and safety. A bad gradient step in an LLM wastes compute; a bad action on hardware breaks the robot or worse. Exploration is not free.Real time, partial observability, embodiment diversity. Policies must run at control rates on onboard compute, from incomplete observations, across robots with different bodies (\\\"embodiments\\\").1.3The paradigm mapEverything in the next eleven weeks fits one taxonomy. Where does the supervision signal come from?ParadigmSignalCore strengthCore weaknessLectureModel-based controlPhysics equationsPrecision, guaranteesNeeds accurate models; brittle in the open worldL2Imitation learningExpert demonstrationsSimple, stable training; safeCapped at expert quality; distribution shift; demos are expensiveL3Reinforcement learningReward functionCan exceed any expert; discovers novel behaviorSample-hungry; reward design; exploration riskL4–L5Offline RLReward labels on a fixed datasetRL's optimization without online riskCan't query actions outside the data (extrapolation error)L5Foundation models / VLAsInternet pretraining + multi-robot dataGeneralization, language interface, common senseYoung; evaluation hard; still data-boundL7–L10Pick a paradigm by what supervision you haveThe table above is really a decision procedure. Robot learning isn't a menu you pick from by taste — the kind of supervision signal you can get forces the choice. Answer the questions below and watch the tree route you to the paradigm (and the lecture) that fits. The point isn't the answer; it's internalizing the questions, because every real project starts here.INTERACTIVE Which paradigm? Follow the supervision signalTap the answers. The highlighted leaf is the paradigm the field would reach for — and the lecture that teaches it. Notice how often the answer is \\\"it depends on the data you can afford,\\\" which is the course's central economic fact in miniature.And the historical arc you should keep in your head — the field's \\\"three eras\\\" (you'll hear the guest lecturer Ted Xiao frame it this way in week 7): (1) task-specific learned components inside classical stacks, (2) end-to-end policies per robot/task family trained by IL or RL, (3) the current bet — robot foundation models: one large model, many robots, many tasks, fueled by internet-scale pretraining. The course's title is literally this arc.Why now? The three converging forcesPriors: vision-language models supply semantic understanding robots could never learn from their own data alone. Data: large multi-robot demonstration corpora (e.g. Open X-Embodiment) and cheap teleoperation rigs. Architectures & recipes: transformers and diffusion models turned out to fit action generation beautifully (L6–L7). The 2022–2026 explosion this course documents is these three arriving together.Bridge · From your backgroundTrain/test split, generalization gap→Open-world deployment: the \\\"test set\\\" is reality, it's non-stationary, and the agent's own actions generate itFeature engineering → deep learning (the ML story you lived through)→Pipeline engineering → end-to-end policies: the same transition, one decade later, with motors attachedModel risk: a pricing model misused outside its data→Distribution shift on hardware: a policy queried outside its training distribution — same failure, faster consequences1.4Orientation for the rest of the courseTwo habits to adopt now. First, for every method you meet, ask the taxonomy questions: What's the supervision signal? On-policy or off? Model-free or model-based? What breaks it? Second, track the data story: nearly every architectural choice in L6–L10 (action chunking, diffusion heads, tokenization, VLM backbones) is ultimately an answer to \\\"how do we squeeze more competence from scarce robot data?\\\"Which is the most fundamental reason robot learning can't simply reuse the vision/NLP playbook wholesale?ARobots need C++ instead of PythonBNeural networks can't output continuous valuesCThere's no web-scale corpus of robot action data, and collecting it requires acting in the physical worldDRobot tasks have no objective functionsC. Vision/NLP scaled on free internet data. Robot experience must be generated — by teleoperators, simulators, or risky autonomous interaction. This scarcity shapes the entire field, from imitation learning's popularity to the VLA bet of importing internet knowledge.A factory robot places the same part on the same fixture 10,000×/day. A startup wants a robot to tidy arbitrary homes. Which statement best reflects the lecture's view?ABoth need learning; classical control is obsoleteBThe factory is well served classically; the home's diversity and contact-richness is where learning earns its keepCThe home robot just needs better camerasDNeither needs learning if the dynamics equations are knownB. Structured, repeatable, instrumented settings are exactly where model-based methods excel. Learning targets the long tail: unmodeled diversity, perception-in-clutter, and contact — the home, not the fixture.Moravec's paradox predicts which of these orderings of difficulty for machines?AFolding laundry < proving theorems < playing GoBAll sensorimotor and symbolic tasks are equally hardCPlaying Go > folding laundry, because Go has more statesDProving theorems and Go fell first; folding laundry remains frontierD. Abstract reasoning yielded to computation early; everyday sensorimotor skill — perception + dexterity under contact — remains hardest, because evolution optimized it for eons and our introspection hides its difficulty.Lecture 1 resourcesSlideslecture1_intro.pdfRecordingETH video portal — Lecture 1 · YouTube playlistHomeworkHW1: PyTorch & NumPy tutorial — do it even if PyTorch is familiar; it calibrates you to the course's conventionsMark L01 complete← BACKNEXT →\",\"l2\":\"LECTURE 02 · FEB 23Robot Control & MDPsFrom feedback control through optimal control (LQR) to the Markov Decision Process — the formal language every later lecture is written in — and the dynamic programming algorithms that solve it exactly when the model is known.Prereqs linear algebra · basic probabilityTime ~45 minWatch first §2.4 + the value-iteration labTools none — paper & the labWatch out forThe reward is not the goal — it's a proxy you designed. Reward ≠ task, and the gap is where things go wrong (§2.5).A known model (LQR/MPC) is frequently better than learning — don't reach for RL reflexively.V and Q are expectations, not guarantees about any single episode.2.1Feedback control: the floor everything stands onBefore any learning: how do classical engineers make a motor follow a target? Feedback. Measure error e(t)=qtarget​−q(t), command effort against it. The PID controller:τ(t)=Kp​e(t)+Ki​∫0t​e(s)ds+Kd​e˙(t)Proportional pushes toward the target, integral kills steady-state offset, derivative damps overshoot. No model required — which is why PID runs the world's machinery. Its limit: it's reactive and single-axis; it doesn't anticipate dynamics, coordinate joints optimally, or trade off competing goals. For that, we make control an optimization problem.Tune the three gains and feel the tradeoffsPID is three knobs, and the only way to understand them is to turn them. Below, a mass must reach the dashed target line; the controller pushes it using the error, the error's integral, and its derivative. Crank Kp​ and it gets there faster but overshoots and rings; add Kd​ and the ringing damps out; if it settles just short of the line, a touch of Ki​ erases that steady-state gap. Push any one too far and watch it go unstable — the entire art of classical control, in one plot.INTERACTIVE PID control: the three gains, feltKp​ = how hard to push on the current error (fast but oscillatory), Kd​ = push against the rate of change (damping), Ki​ = accumulate past error (kills the final offset). The dashed line is the target; the curve is the response over time. There's no single right answer — only tradeoffs, which is exactly why the next step is to make it an optimization (LQR).2.2Optimal control and LQR: control as optimizationPose the goal as minimizing cumulative cost over a horizon, subject to dynamics:u0​,…,uT−1​min​t=0∑T−1​c(xt​,ut​)s.t.xt+1​=f(xt​,ut​)The crown-jewel solvable case is the Linear Quadratic Regulator: linear dynamics xt+1​=Axt​+But​, quadratic cost c=x⊤Qx+u⊤Ru (Q: how much you hate state error; R: how much you hate effort). Solving backward in time via dynamic programming, the optimal cost-to-go stays quadratic, Vt​(x)=x⊤Pt​x, with Pt​ given by the Riccati recursion, and the optimal control is linear state feedback:ut​=−Kt​xt​,Kt​=(R+B⊤Pt+1​B)−1B⊤Pt+1​AThree lessons to carry forward. (1) Cost-to-go is a value function — LQR's Vt​(x) is the exact ancestor of RL's V(s), and the backward Riccati sweep is Bellman recursion in closed form. (2) Nonlinear systems are handled by iteratively linearizing around a trajectory (iLQR) or re-solving a short horizon every step (MPC, model-predictive control — plan, execute one step, replan). (3) All of it presumes f is known. Unknown or unmodelable f is the door RL walks through.2.3The MDP: optimal control meets probabilityGeneralize: stochastic dynamics, general rewards, infinite horizon. A Markov Decision Process is the tuple (S,A,P,r,γ): states, actions, transition kernel P(s′∣s,a), reward r(s,a), discount γ. The Markov property — the future depends on the past only through the current state — is what makes recursion (and hence everything) work. Objective: find π maximizing Vπ(s)=E[∑t​γtrt​∣s0​=s].Two Bellman equations to internalize. For a fixed policy (evaluation):Vπ(s)=a∑​π(a∣s)[r(s,a)+γs′∑​P(s′∣s,a)Vπ(s′)]And the Bellman optimality equation, where evaluation becomes choice:V∗(s)=amax​[r(s,a)+γs′∑​P(s′∣s,a)V∗(s′)]IN WORDS the best you can do from a state is: pick the action that maximizes immediate reward plus the discounted best-you-can-do from wherever it lands you. The value of a state is defined in terms of the values of its successors — a recursion, not a forecast.A foundational (and lovely) fact: every finite MDP has at least one optimal policy that is deterministic and stationary, and acting greedily with respect to Q∗ — π∗(s)=argmaxa​Q∗(s,a) — is optimal. Knowing Q∗ means the decision problem is solved by a lookup.2.4Solving a known MDP: dynamic programmingValue iteration turns the optimality equation into an update: Vk+1​(s)←maxa​[r(s,a)+γ∑s′​P(s′∣s,a)Vk​(s′)], repeated until convergence. Policy iteration alternates full evaluation of the current policy (solve the linear system above) with greedy improvement πk+1​(s)=argmaxa​Qπk​(s,a); the policy improvement theorem guarantees each step is no worse, so it climbs monotonically to π∗ in finitely many steps.Why does value iteration converge, from any initialization? Because the Bellman optimality operator T is a γ-contraction in the sup-norm:∥TV−TW∥∞​≤γ∥V−W∥∞​IN WORDS one sweep of the Bellman update shrinks the worst-case error in your value estimate by at least a factor of γ. Repeat and the error is crushed geometrically toward zero — so the iteration must converge to one specific answer, regardless of where you started.By the Banach fixed-point theorem, T has a unique fixed point — V∗ — and iteration converges geometrically at rate γ. This is the cleanest theorem in the course; savor it, because the moment we replace tables with neural networks (L4), the contraction guarantee evaporates and stability becomes an empirical art.Going deeper: proof of the contraction (it's four lines)For any s: ∣TV(s)−TW(s)∣=​maxa​[r+γEs′​V(s′)]−maxb​[r+γEs′​W(s′)]​.Using ∣maxa​f(a)−maxa​g(a)∣≤maxa​∣f(a)−g(a)∣, this is at most maxa​γ​Es′∣s,a​[V(s′)−W(s′)]​≤γmaxs′​∣V(s′)−W(s′)∣=γ∥V−W∥∞​. Take the sup over s. ∎Note what powered it: γ<1 and the expectation being an average (so it can't exceed the max). The same γ that actuaries use to make EPVs finite is here making an algorithm converge.Watch dynamic programming actually happenThe equations above can feel inert. So let's not solve the MDP in closed form — let's watch value iteration converge. Below is the classic gridworld: a +1 goal, a −1 pit, one wall. Every cell starts believing it's worth 0. Press Step to apply the Bellman optimality update everywhere at once — each cell looks at its neighbors and keeps the best (reward + discounted neighbor value). Watch value bleed outward from the goal, one ring per sweep, and watch the greedy policy arrows snap into a coherent plan the instant the numbers beneath them make sense. That outward propagation is the γ-contraction (proof in the next panel), made visible.INTERACTIVE Value iteration converging on a gridworldCell color = current value estimate (green positive, red negative); cyan arrow = the greedy action it implies. Step does one synchronous Bellman sweep; Run animates to convergence. Notice the policy is correct near the goal long before distant values settle — and that raising γ lets the goal's influence reach farther. Convention: deterministic moves, a per-step living cost, state-rewards with terminals pinned at ±1 — chosen for a clean, readable backup; the classic Russell–Norvig version adds 80/10/10 slip noise.2.5Reward design and the limits of the formalismThe MDP assumes someone hands you r(s,a). In robotics, you are that someone, and it's treacherous: sparse rewards (\\\"+1 on task success\\\") are honest but nearly unlearnable from scratch; dense shaped rewards (\\\"negative distance to goal + bonus for grasp + penalty for jerk...\\\") are learnable but get gamed — the agent optimizes what you wrote, not what you meant. Hold this thought: it returns in L5 (Eureka: LLMs writing reward code) and is one reason imitation (L3) and foundation-model approaches (L9) are so attractive — they sidestep reward design entirely.Bridge · From your backgroundMarkov chains + a decision at each step (your multi-state models, but you choose the transition matrix column)→The MDP, exactly. Remove the actions and an MDP under a fixed policy is a Markov reward process — the object you've always pricedBackward recursion: compute year-T values, discount back (reserves, lattice/binomial option pricing)→Value iteration / Riccati sweep — same backward induction; LQR is your binomial lattice with Gaussians and quadraticsSolving linear systems x=b+Ax→Policy evaluation: Vπ=rπ+γPπVπ⇒Vπ=(I−γPπ)−1rπ; invertibility holds because γ<1 keeps the spectral radius below 1Banach fixed point / geometric series intuition→Why value iteration converges and why error shrinks by factor γ per sweep2.6The papers, decodedWeek 2's discussion papers are a deliberate cold shower before the deep-RL enthusiasm begins:Simple random search provides a competitive approach to RLMania, Guy & Recht · 2018Augmented Random Search — perturb the weights of a linear policy, keep what helps — matches sophisticated deep RL on standard locomotion benchmarks. Moral: the benchmarks were weaker than believed, and baseline discipline matters. When a complicated method wins, always ask what the dumbest competitor scores.Deep Reinforcement Learning Doesn't Work YetIrpan · 2018 (blog essay)The field's most-cited reality check: deep RL is sample-hungry, seed-sensitive, reward-hackable, and often loses to domain-specific methods. Read it as a checklist of failure modes; much of L5–L9 is the field's decade-long answer to this essay.Curiosity-driven Exploration by Self-supervised PredictionPathak et al. · 2017When rewards are absent or sparse, manufacture your own: reward the agent where its learned forward model predicts poorly (\\\"curiosity\\\"), with prediction done in a learned feature space so noise isn't intrinsically fascinating. Your first taste of intrinsic motivation — and of learned models as tools, foreshadowing L8.Policy evaluation for fixed π reduces to Vπ=(I−γPπ)−1rπ. Why is the inverse guaranteed to exist?APπ is symmetricBPπ is a stochastic matrix (spectral radius 1), so γPπ has spectral radius γ<1, making I−γPπ invertibleCRewards are boundedDIt isn't guaranteed; one must check case by caseB. Eigenvalues of γPπ sit inside the disk of radius γ, so 1 is never an eigenvalue and the Neumann series ∑k​(γPπ)k converges to the inverse — the matrix form of \\\"the discounted sum of a probability-weighted future is finite.\\\" Same reason your EPVs converge.What does LQR contribute conceptually to the RL story, beyond being a classical algorithm?AIt shows feedback is unnecessary when models are knownBIt proves all control problems have linear solutionsCIt exhibits a value function (quadratic cost-to-go) computed by exact Bellman backward recursion — the template RL approximates when models are unknownDIt eliminates the need for a reward functionC. LQR is the rare case where the Bellman recursion stays in closed form (quadratics in, quadratics out). RL = the same conceptual objects (value, greedy improvement) when f is unknown/nonlinear and you only get samples.Your shaped reward for \\\"walk forward\\\" includes a bonus proportional to forward velocity of the torso. The learned policy dives headfirst repeatedly. What happened?AReward hacking: diving maximizes the written signal (brief high torso velocity) without achieving the intent (sustained locomotion)BThe discount factor was too lowCValue iteration failed to convergeDThe Markov property was violatedA. Classic specification gaming: the optimizer is faithful to your proxy, not your intent. Fixes include termination penalties, survival bonuses, better shaping — or, per L5's Eureka paper, letting an LLM iterate on the reward code against rollout feedback.Lecture 2 resourcesSlideslecture2_control_mdp.pdfRecordingETH video portal — Lecture 2GuestAbhishek Gupta (UW) — guest spotlightHomeworkHW2: Robot Control & MDPs — implement value/policy iteration yourself; it makes L4 trivial to followMark L02 complete← BACKNEXT →\",\"l3\":\"LECTURE 03 · MAR 02Imitation LearningThe simplest idea in robot learning — copy the expert — and the beautiful, treacherous gap between supervised learning and sequential decision making that it exposes.Prereqs supervised learning · MLETime ~40 minWatch first the compounding-error labs (§3.2)Tools PyTorch · a demo datasetWatch out forHigh held-out accuracy does not imply good rollouts — accuracy is measured on the expert's distribution, not the one the policy induces.BC has no reward function; it's pure supervised learning.The mean of two valid actions can be an invalid action (the averaging trap).3.1Behavioral cloning: it's just MLECollect demonstrations D={(si​,aiexpert​)} — a human teleoperates the robot; you log observations and actions. Behavioral cloning (BC) fits a policy by maximum likelihood:θ∗=argθmax​(s,a)∈D∑​logπθ​(a∣s)For a Gaussian policy with fixed variance this is literally mean-squared-error regression onto expert actions; for discretized actions it's cross-entropy. Everything you know about supervised learning applies: architectures, augmentation, regularization. No reward function, no exploration, no instability. BC is the workhorse that quietly powers most of L6–L9's \\\"policies trained on demonstrations.\\\" So what's the catch?3.2The catch: compounding errorsBC trains on the expert's state distribution dπ∗ but is executed under its own distribution dπθ​. A small error steers the robot to a state slightly off the demonstration manifold — a state the expert never visited, so the policy has no idea what to do there — producing a bigger error, and the trajectory spirals away. Supervised learning's i.i.d. assumption is violated by the policy itself. This is covariate shift where the model causes the shift.Ross & Bagnell made it quantitative. Suppose the learned policy errs with probability ≤ϵ on the expert's distribution. Over a horizon of T steps:J(πBC​)−J(π∗)=O(ϵT2)(worst case)Quadratic in horizon — versus the O(ϵT) you'd naively expect from T independent chances to err.Going deeper: where the T2 comes from (sketch you can reconstruct)Think of the worst case: the first mistake (probability ≤ϵ per step while still on-distribution) may throw the agent into states where it has no guarantees — adversarially, it can incur maximal per-step regret (bounded by 1, say) for all remaining steps. A mistake at step t thus costs up to T−t. Summing expected damage: ∑t=1T​ϵ(T−t)=O(ϵT2). The construction is tight: there exist MDPs achieving it. The lesson isn't the constant — it's the mechanism: off-distribution states have unbounded downstream cost, so per-step accuracy on the demo distribution is the wrong currency. Long-horizon tasks amplify small cloning errors quadratically; this single fact motivates DAgger (below), action chunking (L6–L7), and much else.See the spiral, then see why it's quadraticThe O(ϵT2) bound is the most important inequality in imitation learning, and it's worth feeling in two steps. First, the mechanism: a cloned policy is fine until one small error nudges it off the demonstrated path — and then it's in a state the expert never visited, so there's no one to copy, and it drifts further. Resample the rollouts below and watch a tight bundle fan into a divergent spray as the per-step error ϵ grows.INTERACTIVE Distribution shift: trajectories drifting off the demoThe cyan dashed line is the expert's path. Each orange line is one rollout of the cloned policy: once a small error knocks it off-distribution, there's no correcting signal, so it walks away. Raise ϵ and the funnel widens fast.Second, the scaling: why T2 and not T? Because a mistake at step t doesn't cost one step — it can cost all T−t remaining steps, since the policy may never recover. Summing that triangular cost across the episode gives ∑t​(T−t)∼T2. DAgger relabels the drifted states with expert actions, capping the damage at O(ϵT). Slide ϵ and T and read the gap between the two curves directly.INTERACTIVE Why the error compounds quadraticallySame per-step error ϵ, same constant — the only difference is recovery. Behavioral cloning's regret grows like ϵT2 (red); DAgger's like ϵT (green). At horizon T, cloning is worse by a factor of T — which is why long-horizon tasks punish naïve BC so severely.3.3DAgger: fix the distribution, not the modelDataset Aggregation attacks the mismatch directly: train on the distribution the learner actually induces.DAGGER LOOP 1. π₁ ← BC on expert demos D 2. for k = 1, 2, ... roll out π_k → visit states s ~ d^{π_k} ← learner drives query expert for labels a* at those states ← expert corrects D ← D ∪ {(s, a*)}; π_{k+1} ← train on D ← aggregate, refitFIG. 3.1 — The learner generates the states; the expert supplies the answers. Distribution mismatch dissolves by construction.Under a no-regret online-learning analysis (each round is an online classification problem on the evolving distribution), DAgger achieves regret linear in horizon, O(ϵT) (up to recoverability constants) — the best you could hope for. Its practical sin: it needs an expert on call to label arbitrary mid-rollout states, which is expensive and awkward for humans (labeling \\\"what would I have done here?\\\" out of context is hard). Real systems often use softer cousins: human interventions during rollouts (take over when it drifts, log the corrections) — you'll meet this as HG-DAgger-style data collection and again in HIL-SERL (L4 papers).3.4Two subtler failure modesMultimodality. Human demos are inconsistent: facing an obstacle, half the demos go left, half right. A unimodal policy (Gaussian / MSE regression) learns the mean — straight into the obstacle. Averaging valid answers produces an invalid one. Remember this as the founding problem of Lecture 6: diffusion and energy-based policies exist precisely to represent multimodal p(a∣s).Causal confusion. More data and richer observations can make BC worse. The canonical example (de Haan et al.): a dashboard camera shows the car's own brake light; the network learns \\\"brake light on → brake\\\" — perfect training accuracy by exploiting an effect of the expert's action as if it were a cause. At test time it never initiates braking. Cloning fits correlations in p(a∣s); it has no concept of intervention. Mitigations: hide nuisance channels, careful observation design, targeted interventions to break spurious correlates.The averaging trap, in one pictureHere's the multimodality failure with nothing hidden. The demonstrations cluster into two equally valid maneuvers — swerve left, swerve right — with an obstacle dead center. A mean-squared-error policy minimizes average distance to all the data at once, so its single best answer is the average of left and right: straight ahead, into the obstacle. Toggle between the two policy classes and watch where each one actually sends the robot.INTERACTIVE Why regression-to-the-mean crashes the robotTwo valid modes in the demos (cyan). The MSE policy predicts their mean — a confident steer straight into the obstacle. A generative policy instead samples one mode and commits. This single picture is the entire motivation for Lecture 6: stop predicting one action; model the distribution p(a∣o) and sample.Causal confusion: when more information hurtsThe other subtle failure is stranger, because it runs against every instinct: giving the policy richer observations can make it worse. The toggle below is the canonical dashcam example. Add the car's own brake-light to the observation and training accuracy jumps to a perfect 100% — the network simply reads \\\"brake-light on → brake,\\\" an effect of the expert's action it mistakes for a cause. Then deploy it: there's no expert brake-light to copy, so it never initiates braking. Remove the shortcut and accuracy drops slightly, but the policy is forced to learn the real cause and actually drives.INTERACTIVE The brake-light trap: train accuracy ↑, real driving ↓Cloning fits correlations in p(a∣o); it has no notion of intervention, so it cannot tell a cause from its effect. The fix isn't more data — it's removing the leaked channel (or breaking the correlation with targeted interventions). This is why richer sensors sometimes need careful observation design, not just bigger networks.3.5What actually makes visual BC workModern practice, distilled: (1) Representations carry you. Pretrained or self-supervised visual encoders (the Pari et al. paper below shows BYOL features + literal nearest-neighbor lookup of demo actions is a strong policy) — most of the problem is perception. (2) Inductive bias buys data efficiency. Transporter Networks pose pick-and-place as \\\"match a region to a placement\\\" with translation-equivariant convolutions — tens of demos instead of thousands, by baking spatial structure into the architecture. (3) Demo collection quality dominates. Smooth, consistent, well-covered teleoperation data beats clever algorithms; interfaces (VR rigs, puppeteering setups like ALOHA in L7, handheld grippers like UMI in L6) are first-class research.Bridge · From your backgroundCovariate shift (train/test feature distributions differ)→Compounding errors — same concept with a twist: the model's own outputs generate the shifted inputs, creating a feedback loop with O(T2) damageMLE / cross-entropy training→BC objective max∑logπθ​(a∗∣s) — identical machinery; keep ∇θ​logπθ​ warm for L5Regression to the mean (a phrase you've used professionally)→The multimodality failure: MSE on multimodal targets predicts the mean — here the mean is a crashCorrelation ≠ causation; proxy variables leaking the label→Causal confusion — the brake light is target leakage with motors attachedActive learning (query labels where the model is used/uncertain)→DAgger — active learning where \\\"where the model is used\\\" means the states its own rollouts visit3.6The papers, decodedCausal Confusion in Imitation Learningde Haan, Jayaraman & Levine · 2019Formalizes §3.4's pathology and shows the paradox experimentally: more information in the observation can hurt. Proposes learning over causal graphs + targeted interventions to disambiguate. Read for the framing; the brake-light example will live in your head permanently.The Surprising Effectiveness of Representation Learning for Visual ImitationPari, Shafiullah, Arunachalam & Pinto · 2021 (VINN)Decouples perception from control: self-supervised visual features (BYOL), then k-nearest-neighbor over demo states to pick actions. No policy network at all — and it's competitive. Moral: in visual imitation, representation quality is most of the battle.Transporter Networks: Rearranging the Visual World for Robotic ManipulationZeng et al. · 2020Action = (where to pick, where to place) over a top-down image; exploit translational equivariance by convolving a cropped feature of the pick region over the scene to score placements. Spatial structure as inductive bias → order-of-magnitude better sample efficiency. The counterpoint to \\\"just scale it\\\" — architecture priors still matter when demos are scarce.BC achieves 99% per-step accuracy on held-out expert states for a 500-step task, yet full rollouts almost always fail. The most likely explanation:AThe network is underfittingBThe horizon is too short for errors to matterCEarly small errors push the robot off the demo distribution, where accuracy guarantees say nothing and errors compoundDThe reward function is misspecifiedC. Held-out accuracy is measured on dπ∗; execution happens on dπθ​. With 500 steps, even tiny drift probabilities compound — the O(ϵT2) mechanism. (D is a distractor: BC has no reward function.)What is the essential ingredient DAgger adds over BC, stated abstractly?AA bigger network trained longerBTraining data drawn from the learner's state distribution, labeled by the expertCA learned reward replacing expert labelsDLower variance gradientsB. DAgger changes whose distribution generates training states — from expert's to learner's — eliminating the mismatch by construction and cutting worst-case regret from O(ϵT2) to O(ϵT). Cost: an expert available for on-demand labeling.Demos avoid a pillar by going left 50% and right 50%. A deterministic MSE-trained policy will most likely:AChoose left or right at random each episodeBPick whichever side was marginally more commonCRefuse to moveDOutput the average of the two maneuvers and head toward the pillarD. MSE-optimal prediction is the conditional mean; the mean of \\\"veer left\\\" and \\\"veer right\\\" is \\\"straight ahead.\\\" Multimodal action distributions need multimodal policy classes — mixtures, EBMs, or diffusion (L6).Lecture 3 resourcesSlideslecture3_imitation.pdfRecordingETH video portal — Lecture 3GuestDanfei Xu (Georgia Tech) — guest spotlightHomeworkHW3: Imitation Learning — you will see compounding errors in your own rollouts; nothing teaches §3.2 betterMark L03 complete← BACKNEXT →\",\"l4\":\"LECTURE 04 · MAR 09Reinforcement Learning IDropping the known model: learning value functions from sampled experience. Monte Carlo vs. temporal difference, Q-learning, and the deep-learning surgery (DQN) that made it work at scale — plus why continuous robot actions demand the next lecture.Prereqs L2 · probability · gradient descentTime ~50 minWatch first the gridworld + MC-vs-TD labsTools Gymnasium · PyTorch (for DQN)Watch out forThe critic doesn't pick actions — it evaluates them. The policy/argmax acts.Bootstrapping isn't cheating: it's biased but usually lower-variance and faster.Off-policy learning is precisely what makes replay buffers and learning-from-demos legal — and it's the root of the deadly triad.4.1The new regime: model-freeLecture 2 assumed P(s′∣s,a) on paper, so we could compute expectations. Now the model is gone; the agent only gets samples — transitions (s,a,r,s′) from acting in the world. The questions split as before: prediction (estimate Vπ for a given π) and control (find π∗). The deep idea of this lecture: every dynamic-programming expectation from L2 can be replaced by a sample average or a stochastic step — at the price of variance, bias, and new stability questions.4.2Prediction: Monte Carlo vs. temporal differenceMonte Carlo (MC): run full episodes, observe actual returns Gt​, and update toward them: V(st​)←V(st​)+α(Gt​−V(st​)). Unbiased — Gt​ really is a draw of the quantity Vπ defines — but high variance (a whole episode's randomness piles into one number) and only usable at episode end.Temporal difference (TD(0)): update after every step, toward a bootstrapped target:V(st​)←V(st​)+αδt​, the TD error[rt​+γV(st+1​)−V(st​)]​​The target rt​+γV(st+1​) is the Bellman equation with the expectation replaced by one sample and Vπ replaced by the current estimate. Using your own estimate inside your own target is called bootstrapping. It slashes variance (only one step of randomness) and works online — at the cost of bias while V is wrong. The MC↔TD axis is a pure bias–variance tradeoff, and n-step targets rt​+γrt+1​+⋯+γnV(st+n​) interpolate it. File this away: Lecture 5's GAE is exactly this dial, applied to advantages.Watch unbiased-but-noisy race biased-but-stableThe bias–variance story is easier to believe once you watch it. Below, a fixed 5-state chain has true values we know; press Run episodes and watch two estimators chase them. Monte Carlo (orange) jumps around the truth — unbiased, but every episode injects a whole trajectory's noise. TD (cyan) glides in smoothly — low variance — but leans early on its own wrong guesses (bias). Neither is \\\"right\\\"; they sit at two ends of one ruler.INTERACTIVE Monte Carlo vs. TD: the same target, two error profilesDashed line = true value of each state. Run episodes and watch the bars converge: MC (orange) is jumpy and unbiased; TD (cyan) is smooth but biased while learning. Raise the learning rate α to trade stability for speed. This is the dial GAE will later make continuous.4.3Control: Q-learning and SARSAFor control without a model, learn Q instead of V — then greedy action needs no P: π(s)=argmaxa​Q(s,a). Two classic updates, one crucial difference:SARSA (on-policy):Q(s,a)←Q(s,a)+α[r+γQ(s′,a′)−Q(s,a)],a′∼π(⋅∣s′)Q-learning (off-policy):Q(s,a)←Q(s,a)+α[r+γa′max​Q(s′,a′)−Q(s,a)]SARSA evaluates the policy it's running (including its exploration); Q-learning's max evaluates the greedy policy regardless of how data was collected — it learns about the optimal policy from anyone's experience. That off-policy property is gold (reuse old data, learn from demos, replay buffers) and, simultaneously, the root of the stability problems below. With tabular values, decaying α, and sufficient exploration, Q-learning provably converges to Q∗.Exploration enters here: act greedily and you may never visit the states that would correct your Q. The simplest fix, ϵ-greedy (random action with probability ϵ), is shockingly persistent in practice; alternatives add optimism bonuses, entropy (L5's SAC), or intrinsic curiosity rewards (the Pathak paper from week 2).Going deeper: tabular Q-learning, complete, in ~15 lines — no magicThe fastest way to demystify this lecture is to see that the entire algorithm fits on a notecard. The \\\"brain\\\" is literally a NumPy array.import numpy as np Q = np.zeros((n_states, n_actions)) # the whole agent: one table of guesses for episode in range(50_000): s, done = env.reset(), False while not done: # epsilon-greedy: mostly act on current beliefs, sometimes explore if np.random.rand() < eps: a = np.random.randint(n_actions) else: a = np.argmax(Q[s]) s2, r, done = env.step(a) # one touch of the real world # the heart of Q-learning — one line: target = r + gamma * np.max(Q[s2]) * (1 - done) Q[s, a] += alpha * (target - Q[s, a]) s = s2Read the update line slowly. target is the Bellman optimality equation with the expectation replaced by the one transition we just experienced, and Q∗ replaced by our current guess (bootstrapping). (1 - done) zeroes the future at terminal states — death is an absorbing state of value 0, exactly your annuity recursion's boundary condition. The += alpha * (error) is the credibility blend. That's it — no other state, no other math. Every deep value-based method (DQN and descendants) is this loop with the table swapped for a neural network — plus the scaffolding (replay buffer, target network) required to survive that swap, which is precisely what §4.4 explains.Why exploration is its own problemQ-learning's convergence guarantee assumes you keep visiting every state-action — but a greedy agent stops exploring the moment it finds something decent, and may never discover the better option two doors down. The cleanest sandbox is a multi-armed bandit: several actions with hidden payoffs, and only your own pulls to learn from. Try the three classic strategies and watch the regret (lost reward vs. always picking the best arm) accumulate.INTERACTIVE Exploration strategies on a multi-armed banditEach bar is an arm; height = your current estimate, the dot = its hidden true payoff. Greedy often locks onto a wrong arm; ε-greedy keeps sampling and usually finds the best; UCB explores by optimism (try what you're uncertain about). Watch cumulative regret — flatter is better. This is the §4.3 exploration problem in its purest form.4.4Function approximation and the DQN surgeryRobots don't have tabular states — observations are images in Rmillions. Replace the table with a network Qϕ​(s,a) trained by regression on TD targets:L(ϕ)=E(s,a,r,s′)∼D​[(targetr+γa′max​Qϕ−​(s′,a′)​​−Qϕ​(s,a))2]IN WORDS it's plain regression with a moving target: train Q to predict \\\"reward now plus discounted best next-state value.\\\" The label is built from a frozen copy Qϕ−​ so you're not chasing your own tail every step — the trick that keeps the deadly triad from exploding.Naively this diverges, for a reason worth understanding: it's not true gradient descent on any fixed objective — the target moves with the parameters, the data distribution moves with the policy, and bootstrapped errors feed back. The folklore name for the unstable cocktail is the deadly triad: function approximation + bootstrapping + off-policy data. DQN's two famous stabilizers, visible in the loss above:Replay buffer D: store transitions; train on random minibatches. Breaks temporal correlation (gradient steps see ~i.i.d. data again) and reuses experience — possible only because Q-learning is off-policy.Target network ϕ−: compute targets with a frozen, periodically-updated copy. Stops the regression from chasing its own tail; turns a moving-target problem into a sequence of quasi-stationary ones.One refinement worth knowing by name: the max in the target overestimates values under noise (Jensen-flavored: E[max]≥max[E]); Double DQN decouples action selection (online net) from action evaluation (target net) to debias it.The bias–variance spectrum, concretelyMonte Carlo and TD aren't rival camps; they're the two ends of one ruler, and seeing that makes Lecture 5 almost free. Ask how to estimate V(st​). The one-step (TD) target uses a single real reward, then trusts the current estimate: rt​+γV(st+1​) — low variance (only one noisy reward), but biased (it leans on a guess). The full (MC) target waits for the real return Gt​ — unbiased, but high variance (it absorbs every random reward and transition to the end of the episode). In between sits the n-step target,Gt(n)​=rt​+γrt+1​+⋯+γn−1rt+n−1​+γnV(st+n​).The more real rewards you wait for, the less you lean on the biased estimate and the more real-world noise you let in. That's the entire bias–variance dial — and in Lecture 5 it gets a smooth, continuous knob called GAE-λ. Hold onto this picture; the interactive λ-dial there is the same idea made draggable.Going deeper: a tiny chain where deep Q-learning provably blows up (the deadly triad, concretely)Why is the triad \\\"deadly\\\" rather than merely annoying? Picture Baird's counterexample in miniature: a handful of states whose values share an overlapping linear feature representation, trained off-policy with bootstrapped targets — all three legs present. Each update nudges the weights to shrink one state's TD error, but because the features overlap, the same nudge inflates a neighbor's predicted value. That inflated value becomes part of the next bootstrap target, which justifies inflating the weights again, which inflates the neighbor further. With no environment changing and no reward driving it, the value estimates spiral to infinity — the estimator is chasing its own moving reflection.Remove any single leg and the divergence vanishes: use on-policy data (the visited-state distribution self-corrects), or drop bootstrapping (Monte Carlo targets are anchored to real returns), or use a lookup table instead of function approximation (no cross-state leakage). This is exactly why DQN's two famous tricks target the bootstrapping leg specifically — a replay buffer (decorrelate updates) and a frozen target network (compute maxa′​Q(s′,a′) from an old, held-still copy so the target stops moving while you chase it). They don't make the triad safe in general; they stabilize the one leg you can't remove.Toggle the three legs and watch divergence appearThe claim that the triad is dangerous only when all three legs are present is the kind of thing you should verify, not take on faith. Below is a miniature value-learning problem with three switches: function approximation (shared features across states), bootstrapping (targets built from current estimates), and off-policy data. Flip them on one at a time and the value estimates stay bounded. Flip on all three and watch them spiral to infinity — with no reward driving it, the estimator chasing its own reflection. Remove any single leg and the divergence dies.INTERACTIVE The deadly triad: lethal only in combinationEach line is a state's value estimate over training steps. Any one or two legs: stable. All three: divergence. This is why DQN's tricks (replay buffer, frozen target network) target the bootstrapping leg — it's the one you can't simply remove, so you tame it instead.Everything above leans on maxa​Q(s,a). A robot arm's action is a vector in R7 (or R14+ bimanual) — that max is a nonconvex optimization per decision, per training target. Discretizing 7 dimensions at 10 bins each is 107 actions. Value-based control, as stated, does not survive contact with robot action spaces. Three escapes, and the course takes all of them: (i) restructure the action space so the max is cheap — this week's Zeng et al. paper makes \\\"actions\\\" pixels of a Q-map and the max an argmax over an image; (ii) learn a policy network directly — Lecture 5; (iii) have a policy network propose, a critic evaluate — actor-critic, also Lecture 5.Bridge · From your backgroundEstimating EPVs from experience data without a life table; experience studies→Model-free prediction — MC = \\\"observe completed cohorts, average discounted outcomes\\\"; TD = \\\"update continuously from each period's data\\\"Credibility blending: new estimate = Z⋅observed + (1−Z)⋅prior→Every update this lecture: V←(1−α)V+α⋅target — α is a credibility weight applied per stepExponential smoothing / recursive estimators→TD learning — a smoothed estimator whose \\\"observations\\\" are partly its own previous outputs (bootstrapping); hence both its efficiency and its biasFitting a curve to noisy targets (regression)→Deep Q-learning — regression where the targets are generated by the model being fit, the data by the policy being learned: the deadly triad in one sentence4.6The papers, decodedEvolution Strategies as a Scalable Alternative to Reinforcement LearningSalimans et al. · 2017Skip values and gradients: perturb policy parameters with Gaussian noise across a population, weight perturbations by episode return, step. Embarrassingly parallel (communication = scalar returns), indifferent to reward sparsity within episodes. Conceptual link for L5: ES estimates the same ascent direction as policy gradients, in parameter space instead of action space.Learning Synergies between Pushing and Grasping with Self-supervised Deep RLZeng et al. · 2018Q-learning on a real robot by redesigning the action space: actions = \\\"push/grasp at pixel (x, y) with rotation θ,\\\" Q-function = an image-sized map per primitive, the max = an argmax over pixels. Watch it learn that an unsuccessful-looking push has value because it makes a future grasp possible — long-horizon credit assignment you can see with your eyes.Precise and Dexterous Robotic Manipulation via Human-in-the-Loop Reinforcement LearningLuo et al. · 2024 (HIL-SERL)The modern existence proof that real-world RL works: sample-efficient off-policy RL from images + a few demos + human interventions that both correct the robot and feed the buffer; near-perfect success on genuinely hard tasks (timing-sensitive flipping, dual-arm assembly) in 1–2.5 hours of training. The recipe — off-policy backbone, demos as scaffolding, human corrections — is where value-based methods earn their keep on hardware.Why can DQN use a replay buffer while basic policy-gradient methods (L5 preview) cannot, without correction?AReplay buffers only store imagesBQ-learning's target is off-policy — valid for transitions from any behavior — whereas the vanilla policy gradient is an expectation under the current policyCPolicy gradients don't use neural networksDThey can; it's purely an engineering choiceB. The Q-learning target r+γmaxa′​Q(s′,a′) is a statement about the transition itself, true regardless of who chose a. The policy gradient theorem's expectation is over dπθ​ — old data is the wrong distribution unless importance-weighted (which is exactly the thread PPO pulls in L5).TD(0) vs. Monte Carlo in one line:ATD trades variance for bias by bootstrapping off its own estimate; MC is unbiased but high-variance and episode-boundBTD is unbiased; MC is biasedCThey are identical when γ=1DMC requires a model of P(s′∣s,a)A. MC's target Gt​ is a true sample of the definition (unbiased, variance of a whole episode). TD's target r+γV(st+1​) contains the current estimate — one step of noise (low variance), but biased until V converges. n-step methods interpolate.Why does vanilla value-based control hit a wall on a 7-DoF arm specifically at the action space?ASeven dimensions exceed what neural networks can inputBContinuous actions make rewards undefinedCThe replay buffer can't store real-valued actionsDBoth acting and forming targets require maxa​Q(s,a) — an inner nonconvex optimization over R7 at every stepD. The max is the engine of Q-learning, and it's intractable over continuous high-dimensional actions (discretization explodes exponentially). Escapes: restructure actions (Zeng's pixel-wise Q-maps), or maintain an explicit actor — Lecture 5.Lecture 4 resourcesSlideslecture4_rl_I.pdfRecordingETH video portal — Lecture 4GuestAviral Kumar (CMU / Google DeepMind) — guest spotlightMark L04 complete← BACKNEXT →\"}"